# Processed MedAESQA

Analysis-ready tables derived from `medaesqa_v1.json`. Load these instead of parsing the
raw JSON.

Produced by `notebooks/exploratory-data-analysis/medaesqa_prep.ipynb`.
Source fingerprint and cleaning decisions are recorded in `medaesqa_freeze.txt`.

**What MedAESQA is.** 40 real health questions from MedlinePlus, each answered by one
medical expert and by 30 different AI systems. Medical experts then graded every sentence
and every citation by hand. It is our human-labeled answer key for Verifier calibration
and faithfulness evaluation, not training data.

---

## medaesqa_questions.csv — 40 rows

One row per question.

| Column | Description |
|---|---|
| `question_id` | Question identifier, 116 to 155 |
| `question` | The question as a member of the public asked it |
| `question_focus` | Main clinical entity, e.g. `sleep apnea`. Blank for 1 question in the source file |
| `question_type` | What is being asked, lowercased: treatment, etiology, prognosis, diagnosis, manifestation, and 11 others |
| `task` | Lowercased: decision support, factoid, or effect. The source mixes `Decision Support` and `Decision support` |
| `subject_matter` | clinical / problem, clinical / drug, clinical / intervention, or genetics |
| `body_system_raw` | Body system as written in the source file |
| `specialty_raw` | Specialty as written in the source file |
| `body_systems` | Cleaned, pipe-separated, lowercase |
| `specialties` | Cleaned, pipe-separated, lowercase |
| `n_body_systems` | Count of body systems |
| `n_specialties` | Count of specialties. 7 questions list more than one |
| `expert_answer` | The expert-written answer, with PMIDs in square brackets |
| `expert_answer_n_words` | Word count of the expert answer |
| `expert_answer_n_words_outlier` | True if the length is outside 1.5 x IQR. Flag only |
| `expert_answer_n_words_z` | Z-score of the word count |

**Why the `_raw` columns exist.** The source file packs several values into one string with
inconsistent spacing, e.g. `pulmonology/ ENT / cardiology`. Counting those as a single
category makes common specialties look rarer than they are. We split on `/`, stripped
whitespace and lowercased, and kept the originals so the cleaning can be checked.

One exception: `ob./gyn` is a single specialty that contains a slash. It is mapped to
`ob-gyn` before splitting, so it is not counted as two fake specialties `ob.` and `gyn`.

To count honestly:
```python
questions["specialties"].str.split("|").explode().value_counts()
```

---

## medaesqa_nuggets.csv — 498 rows

One row per expert-curated atomic fact. Gold reference for the Extractor agent.

| Column | Description |
|---|---|
| `question_id` | Links to questions |
| `nugget_idx` | Position within that question's nugget list |
| `nugget` | The fact as written, usually `Predicate(subject, object)` |
| `modality` | Hedge in front of the predicate, e.g. `may`. Null for 329 nuggets with no hedge; 169 (34%) have one |
| `predicate` | Parsed relation, e.g. `Treat`, `Cause`, `Is`. Null when the pattern doesn't fit |
| `arguments` | Parsed contents of the parentheses, CUI tags included |
| `n_arguments` | Number of comma-separated arguments |
| `fits_predicate_pattern` | True for 495, False for 3 |
| `cuis` | UMLS concept IDs, pipe-separated, e.g. `C0870811\|C0037315` |
| `n_cuis` | Count of CUIs. Every nugget has at least one |

Median 11.5 nuggets per question, range 3 to 31. To see the most common predicates:
```python
nuggets["predicate"].value_counts().head(10)
```

**Three findings for the Extractor owner.**

Hedges are common. About a third of nuggets start with a modality like `[may]`, as in
`[may] Prevent (lifestyle changes [C0870811], sleep apnea [C0037315])`. The hedge is kept
in `modality` rather than dropped, because "may prevent" and "prevents" are different
claims and a faithful simplification has to preserve the difference.

CUIs are present. They are written inline after each concept, e.g. `sleep apnea [C0037315]`,
not in a separate field, and are extracted from the text with the pattern `C` + 7 digits.
(An earlier version of these files missed them and reported 0 CUIs everywhere.)

`fits_predicate_pattern` is a loose check. It means the nugget has the form
`Word (...)`, not that it is a clean two-argument relation. Complex nuggets such as
`IF Treat (...) THEN [may] Form (...)` still match, with `predicate` = `IF` and the rest
packed into `arguments`. To find them:
```python
nuggets[nuggets["arguments"].str.contains(r"\)\s*(THEN|AND|OR)\b", na=False)]
```
Any extraction schema needs to handle more than two-argument relations.

---

## medaesqa_sentences.csv — 5,162 rows

One row per sentence of a machine-generated answer. This is the Verifier calibration set.

| Column | Description |
|---|---|
| `question_id` | Links to questions |
| `method_id` | Which AI system produced the answer, M1 to M30 |
| `sentence_idx` | Zero-based position in the answer |
| `sentence_id` | One-based id from the source file |
| `sentence` | The sentence text |
| `relevance` | Human grade. See distribution below |
| `answer_is_accurate` | Whether the whole answer passed a first-pass accuracy check |
| `n_citations` | Citations attached to this sentence |
| `has_relevance_label` | False for 244 rows |
| `sentence_n_words` | Word count of the sentence |
| `sentence_n_words_outlier` | True if the length is outside 1.5 x IQR. Flag only |
| `sentence_n_words_z` | Z-score of the word count |
| `n_citations_z` | Z-score of `n_citations` |

Relevance distribution:

| Label | Count | Share |
|---|---|---|
| required | 3,958 | 76.7% |
| unnecessary | 698 | 13.5% |
| (no label) | 244 | 4.7% |
| borderline | 243 | 4.7% |
| inappropriate | 19 | 0.4% |

**Read this before computing agreement.** All 244 unlabeled sentences come from the four
answers marked `answer_is_accurate = no`. Graders skipped sentence-level work on answers
they had already rejected wholesale. So the labeled pool of 4,918 sentences is
conditioned on the answer having passed a first check, which means the hardest cases may
be underrepresented. Filter on `has_relevance_label` rather than imputing.

**Raw agreement is misleading here.** 80.5% of labeled sentences are `required`, so a
Verifier that outputs `required` for everything without reading scores 80.5%. That is
above the project's 80% agreement target. Report Cohen's kappa instead, which subtracts
out agreement expected by chance.

**The 19 `inappropriate` sentences** are the potentially harmful ones. Small enough to read
in full, and the most important cases in the dataset for error analysis.

---

## medaesqa_citations.csv — 8,124 rows

One row per unique sentence-PMID pair. Join to sentences on
`question_id` + `method_id` + `sentence_idx`.

| Column | Description |
|---|---|
| `question_id` | Links to questions |
| `method_id` | M1 to M30 |
| `sentence_idx` | Links to sentences |
| `pmid` | PubMed ID of the cited paper |
| `evidence_relation` | Whether the paper backs the claim. See below |
| `evidence_excerpt` | Human-written passage justifying the judgment. Blank for 1,959 rows (24.1%) |
| `n_excerpts` | How many excerpts backed this pair, 1 to 7 |
| `excerpt_n_words` | Word count of the excerpt. Null when the excerpt is blank |
| `excerpt_n_words_outlier` | True if the length is outside 1.5 x IQR. Flag only |
| `excerpt_n_words_z` | Z-score of the word count |

Evidence relation distribution:

| Label | Count | Share |
|---|---|---|
| supports | 5,425 | 66.8% |
| not_relevant | 1,550 | 19.1% |
| neutral | 559 | 6.9% |
| invalid citation | 409 | 5.0% |
| contradicts | 181 | 2.2% |

**How duplicates were resolved.** The raw file has 9,611 citation rows because one
sentence-PMID pair can carry several excerpts. In 390 cases those excerpts disagree with
each other. We collapsed to one row per pair, and when verdicts conflict the most severe
wins:

```
contradicts > supports > neutral > not_relevant > invalid citation
```

Contradiction beats support because the source paper's own definition is that a document
supports a claim only when nothing in it contradicts the claim. Picking arbitrarily would
have scored roughly 40 contradictions as supporting.

Those 390 conflicts are also a result in their own right. Roughly 5% of citations have no
single agreed human verdict, which puts a practical ceiling on any Verifier's achievable
agreement.

**Two open questions for the advisor.** We get 8,124 pairs against the paper's 8,111. And
`invalid citation` appears in the file but is not one of the four relations the paper
describes, so we do not know how it was counted in the published totals.

---

## medaesqa_freeze.txt

Source SHA-256, row counts, and every cleaning rule in plain text.

**If the hash changes, every reported number is suspect.** Re-run the prep notebook and
check its validation section before trusting anything downstream.

To verify:
```bash
shasum -a 256 medaesqa_v1.json                          # macOS / Linux
Get-FileHash medaesqa_v1.json -Algorithm SHA256         # Windows PowerShell
```

---

## Validation

The prep notebook checks these against the published paper. All matched:

| Check | Ours | Paper |
|---|---|---|
| Questions | 40 | 40 |
| Answer sentences | 5,162 | 5,162 |
| Sentences graded `required` | 3,958 | 3,958 |
| Evidence excerpts | 7,651 | 7,651 |
| Citations | 8,124 | 8,111 (see above) |

---

## Missing values, outliers, normalization

**Missing values are reported, never filled in.** In this dataset a gap always means either
"a human did not grade this" or "this field does not apply", so imputing would invent labels
the Verifier is later calibrated against. Every gap in the four tables:

| Table | Column | Missing | Why |
|---|---|---|---|
| questions | `question_focus` | 1 (2.5%) | Left blank in the source file |
| nuggets | `modality` | 329 (66.1%) | Nugget has no hedge |
| nuggets | `predicate`, `arguments`, `n_arguments` | 3 (0.6%) | Nugget does not fit `Word (...)` |
| sentences | `relevance` | 244 (4.7%) | Never graded: answer was rejected as inaccurate |
| citations | `evidence_excerpt` | 1,959 (24.1%) | Annotator wrote no excerpt |

**Outliers are flagged, never removed.** The only numeric values are word counts derived
from the text. Anything outside 1.5 x IQR is marked in a `*_outlier` column. Rows are kept
because this is a frozen evaluation set: dropping them would break every comparison with
the paper, and a long sentence is still a real answer the Verifier has to judge. Counts
like `n_citations` are not flagged, because almost every value is 1 and the rule would
mark everything else as an outlier.

| Column | Min | Median | Max | Normal range | Flagged |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| `expert_answer_n_words` | 45 | 88 | 204 | 13.5 to 153.5 | 3 (7.5%) |
| `sentence_n_words` | 1 | 21 | 61 | 3.5 to 39.5 | 124 (2.4%) |
| `excerpt_n_words` | 5 | 26 | 283 | up to 61.5 | 249 (3.1%) |

**Short sentences are mostly junk, not real outliers.** The 1-word "sentences" are leftover
citation placeholders like `[PMID1].` and `[PMID3].`, plus one `..`. Most come from rejected,
ungraded answers, but the `..` (question 145, M5) was graded `unnecessary`. Anyone computing
per-sentence metrics such as readability should filter on `sentence_n_words_outlier` or a
minimum word count first, or these fragments will distort the averages.

**Normalization is optional.** Nothing in the pipeline trains on these counts, so nothing
needs scaling. For anyone who wants to compare them on one scale, z-scored copies
`(x - mean) / std` are in `*_z` columns. The original counts are unchanged, since those are
the numbers that match the paper.

